<p>Thieves stole paintings by Pierre-Auguste Renoir, Paul Cézanne and Henri Matisse from the Magnani Rocca Foundation, a museum near Parma in northern Italy, during a late-night break-in on 22 March, according to police and multiple media reports. Police and news reports said the theft took about three minutes. The stolen works are said to be worth millions, and police are investigating.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/HddpttQyljLSOXq6Sgus-LqxYgBoemZdbEftcKTDOaNBM43I-Owa8YZAkbvoOSF4QcvJrG3PIqZWVl-fzgtAP12QSo1-4koLCVAZZf0V3Bh7rLvtfk4x_WM49u41rU5mKQ_0S4ztdXTb1tdP57Wdm98zefa5xw6z7H8PTn_xAB5qo34nXOqQCOGpmEaNiJbiABwNCGcOtySoHYJfxgV0XdG8JWoq2oX9Yr1tDgnRJUeV9tQwaAkiH_D6eL2CLdhztpFyl2ATGv9ceFBZLA' alt='Exterior view associated with reporting on the theft at the Magnani Rocca Foundation near Parma.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Reporting lag: The case drew wider attention after Rai reported the theft and police confirmed the account, rather than immediately after the break-in.</li><li>Villa museum: The Magnani Rocca Foundation is housed in a villa near Parma, not a major city-center gallery, which shapes how readers picture the setting of the raid.</li><li>Selective haul: DW described the loss as three paintings by French masters, suggesting the intruders targeted a small, high-value group of works.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Italian police: Police said four masked men entered the Magnani Rocca Foundation villa during the night and stole the paintings. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cn4vw2xmpzzo'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>Rai television network: Rai first reported the museum theft, and police later confirmed the account cited by other outlets. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/2026/mar/30/paintings-stolen-museum-italy-renoir-cezanne-matisse'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>News outlets&#x27; assessment: Coverage emphasized that the operation lasted under three minutes, framing the theft as a fast, targeted museum raid. (<a href='